A||N||7||beachbomb||Engine replacement question||||1||
Z||000000||beachbomb||06-17-2001||04:09 PM||||Hey all, i just got back from the bone-yard(see General topic for that) and have a question, will a '87 4 cyclinder engine fit in my '84 Indy(which is also a 4 cyclinder) without much of a problem,are all the connections basically the same, they had a blue '87 coupe where some-one had dropped the engine out of and just took the trans..plmk, thanks..jim(2 '84 Indy)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||06-17-2001||04:19 PM||||I'm pretty sure fitting/bolting is pretty much the same. However, your going to need all the engine components, wireing harness, and computer for the 87 engine, mostly due to the DIS ingnition system.||||reg||1
Z||000002||beachbomb||06-17-2001||08:49 PM||||Good point, thanks alot, i didn't think of that!..Jim||||reg||4
Z||000003||Toddster||06-17-2001||09:06 PM||||There are actally several differences. Even the air cleaner and valve cover are not compatible. Be sure to get the COMPLETE '87 motor, sensors, accesories, and computer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||06-18-2001||10:14 AM||||Toddster is right get the ECM harness and sensors air cleaner, everything you can git your mitts on. ||||reg||14
Z||000005||DJRice||06-18-2001||07:04 PM||||Keep in mind also that the wiring connector block moved from the center of the firewall to the area next to the battery in 1985.  The 87 wiring will likely not reach to the center of the firewall where your current wiring is.  You would need to pull the main wiring harness out of the donor car interior to solve that problem.  (which can be done but its a rather large amount of work).<BR><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||beachbomb||06-18-2001||07:37 PM||||Thanks for all the advice, i think i might just wait until i get the 4 cyclinder..thanks..jim||||reg||4
Z||000007||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-19-2001||12:10 AM||||The 1984 and 1985 Iron Dukes are pretty similar, but as noted above, the wiring changed dramatically to allow the option of a V6 in 1985.<P>The earlier engines (84 and 85) have a lot of advantages when it comes to adding a little more power.  For example, all of the good cam grinds are made only for the early flat tappet hydrallic cam shaft.  The 86 and up engines use a roller hydrallic cam, so you would have to change back to the 84-85 valve train to change the cam.<P>Also, the 87 and 88 Iron Dukes use different TBI and intake.  This means the redesigned Holley TBI (which rocks!) will only fit with a custom adaptor plate.  <P>Those are the two biggest reasons to build a Duke from the early years.  Your Indy needs a good, powerful engine.  I have just built a Super-Duke for my 1984 Sport Coupe.  If you want to know more, email me.||||reg||10
